---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
const title = "How to Convert a Salary to an Hourly Rate - Toolkit Lab";
const description = "How to turn an annual salary into an hourly rate using hours per week and weeks worked, and what the figure leaves out.";
---
<Base title={title} description={description} path="/guides/how-to-convert-a-salary-to-an-hourly-rate.html">
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
  <div class="wrap article">
    <p class="note"><a href="/guides.html">Guides</a> / Pay and work</p>
    <h1>How to Convert a Salary to an Hourly Rate</h1>
    <p class="lead">An hourly rate helps when you compare a salaried job with an hourly one, or check whether an offer fits your time. The calculation is simple, but the assumptions matter.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
<h2>The formula</h2>
<p>Hourly rate = annual salary ÷ (hours per week × weeks worked per year). A salary of 52,000 for a 40-hour week over 52 weeks gives 52,000 ÷ 2,080 = 25.00 per hour.</p>
<h2>Weekly and monthly</h2>
<p>Weekly pay is the salary divided by 52. Monthly pay is the salary divided by 12. These are before tax and deductions.</p>
<h2>What the figure leaves out</h2>
<ul>
<li>Unpaid breaks, commuting time and unpaid overtime lower the real hourly rate.</li>
<li>Paid holidays and benefits are not counted, so a job with more leave can be worth more than its hourly figure suggests.</li>
<li>Tax and pension contributions reduce what you take home.</li>
</ul>
<h2>Try it yourself</h2>
<p>The <a href="/tools/salary-to-hourly-converter.html">Salary to Hourly Pay Converter</a> shows the hourly, weekly and monthly figures from your salary and hours.</p>
    <div class="ad-slot" aria-label="Advertisement"></div>
  </div>
  <aside class="ad-rail" aria-label="Advertisement"><div class="ad-slot ad-slot-rail"></div></aside>
</Base>
